**With CSS to demonstrate the control:**

```css
/* Base reset */
.field-wrapper, .field-inner, .field-content {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

/* Outer spacing */
.field-wrapper {
  margin-bottom: 20px; /* Space between fields */
  padding: 10px;
}

/* Inner div for background/shadow */
.field-inner {
  background: #f9f9f9;
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 15px;
}

/* Flex layout for label/input alignment */
.field-content {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
}

/* Label spans - make them take fixed width */
.label-wrapper {
  flex: 0 0 120px;
  min-width: 0;
}
.label-wrapper, .label-inner {
  display: table-cell;
  vertical-align: middle;
}

/* Input spans - take remaining space */
.input-wrapper {
  flex: 1;
}
.input-wrapper, .input-inner, .input-content {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
}

/* Actual input styling */
input, textarea {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 8px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  border-radius: 4px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

/* Nested spans allow separate padding/margin controls */
.input-inner {
  padding-right: 5px;
}
.input-content {
  padding-left: 5px;
}
```

**Why this works:**
- The outer `<div>`s control section-level spacing (margins, padding, backgrounds)
- The middle `<span>`s give you granular control over label/input alignment
- The inner `<span>`s provide additional precision for padding/margins without affecting the input itself
- Each wrapper adds a hook for CSS targeting without needing extra classes

**Custom spacing examples:**
```css
/* Make only the third field have extra top margin */
.field-wrapper:nth-of-type(3) .field-inner {
  margin-top: 30px;
}

/* Add more space between label and input on mobile */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .field-content {
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 5px;
  }
  .label-wrapper {
    flex-basis: auto;
    width: 100%;
  }
}
```

**Caveat:** While this works, it's generally considered "divitis" (too many unnecessary wrappers). Modern CSS with Grid, Flexbox, or even simple `display: grid` on the form is cleaner. But if you need maximum control for legacy systems or very specific layout quirks, nested wrappers can help. Just remember to use semantic HTML where possible (e.g., actual `<label>` elements) and keep accessibility in mind.

